# CQ 06: Which operators begin a chain, which end one, and which state no order?
#
# The counterpart of question 05. That one reports the order, this one reports
# where it starts and stops, and where it was never stated.
#
# An operator without a predecessor begins a chain and one without a successor
# ends it. Both are ordinary, and each level of a decomposed process has one of
# each.
#
# An operator with neither states no order at all, and whether that is a gap
# depends on where it sits. A decomposed operator carries its order on the
# level below, as O1 of Figure 5 does: the coarse view shows one step, the
# refined view orders the two it consists of. An elementary operator with no
# order is the case worth finding, since nothing else states when it runs.
#
# The two are therefore reported apart. The role is computed rather than
# asserted, so a model states its order once and this answer follows from it.
PREFIX vdi3682: <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#>
SELECT DISTINCT ?operator ?role
WHERE {
  ?operator a vdi3682:ProcessOperator .
  OPTIONAL { ?operator vdi3682:hasPredecessor ?pred }
  OPTIONAL { ?operator vdi3682:hasSuccessor   ?succ }
  BIND(EXISTS { ?operator vdi3682:isComposedOf ?anySub } AS ?isDecomposed)
  BIND(IF(!BOUND(?pred) && !BOUND(?succ),
          IF(?isDecomposed, "no order, ordered one level down", "no order stated"),
       IF(!BOUND(?pred), "starts a chain",
       IF(!BOUND(?succ), "ends a chain", ""))) AS ?role)
  FILTER(?role != "")
} ORDER BY ?role ?operator
